   "url": "https://www.ruf.rice.edu/~eulers/tests/2004/alg-problems.pdf"
  },
  "grade": "9-12",
  "points": 1,
  "timeMinutes": null,
  "type": "self",
  "prompt": "Find all real $x$ such that $\\dfrac{x(x-2)(x-4)}{(x-1)(x-3)(x-5)}<0$.",
  "image": null,
  "choices": null,
  "answer": "$0<x<1$ or $2<x<3$ or $4<x<5$",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Sign chart",
    "body": "The critical points are $0,1,2,3,4,5$, and the expression changes sign at each (all are simple). For $x<0$ numerator and denominator are both negative, so the quotient is positive. Moving right it alternates: negative on $(0,1)$, positive on $(1,2)$, negative on $(2,3)$, positive on $(3,4)$, negative on $(4,5)$, positive for $x>5$. (At 1, 3, 5 it is undefined; at 0, 2, 4 it equals 0.)\n\n**Answer: $0<x<1$, $2<x<3$, $4<x<5$**"
   }
  ],
  "topics": [
   "inequalities",
   "algebra"
  ],
  "difficulty": 1,
